-- title:
--   Continuity of the Riemann zeta function on $\mathbb{C} \setminus \{1\}$
-- statement:
--   The Riemann zeta function $\zeta$ is continuous on the complement of its pole:
--   $$\zeta \in C^{0}\bigl( \mathbb{C} \setminus \{1\} \bigr),$$
--   that is, $\zeta$ is continuous on the set $\mathbb{C} \setminus \{1\}$ (formally, `ContinuousOn` the universal set with the point $1$ removed).
--
--   This is an immediate consequence of the analyticity of the completed zeta function away from its simple pole at $s = 1$, but having it packaged as a standalone continuity statement is convenient for compactness arguments: for example, in the proof that for every $T$ there is a $\sigma < 1$ with no zeros of $\zeta$ in the box $\{\sigma' + it : \sigma' \ge \sigma,\ |t| \le T\}$, one applies continuity of $\zeta$ on a compact set together with the non-vanishing of $\zeta$ on the $1$-line.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/AlexKontorovich/PrimeNumberTheoremAnd/blob/f55e85551ac10e96d98262a354cfcaac2825f2da/PrimeNumberTheoremAnd/ZetaBounds.lean#L2562-L2567

